course: english
lesson: 29
title: Fronted adverbials
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An adverbial is a word or group of words that tells you more about the verb: when, where, how, or how often something happened. «We went home later that day.» When you move it to the very front of the sentence, it becomes a fronted adverbial: «Later that day, we went home.» Then it is followed by a comma, like a little pause before the main news.
explain: Fronted adverbials come in all sizes. One word: «Suddenly, the lights went out.» A phrase: «Under the bridge, the troll waited.» «All of a sudden, the dog sat up.» Even a whole clause can do the job: «Because I was hungry, I ate an apple.» Tyler in Texas asks, «Y'all, "all of a sudden" is a fronted adverbial?» (y'all means you all.) Yes, when it's at the front.
explain: Two traps. First, the comma: «After lunch we played» should be «After lunch, we played.» Second, the rest must still be a full sentence. Ali writes «Carefully, carried the eggs.» Who carried them? The subject is missing: «Carefully, Ali carried the eggs.» Test it by covering the adverbial: what is left must make sense alone.
explain: Use them for variety, not in every sentence. Grace starts every sentence of her story with one, and it sounds like a robot: «Then, I woke up. Next, I ate. After that, I left.» Some adverbials show the speaker's feeling: Dad says, drily, «Frankly, I preferred the grammar of my day.» Mum in Bristol asks, «What's one of those adverbials then, my lover?» (my lover is a friendly West Country way to say love or mate). Her Gen Z daughter says, «Lowkey, fronted adverbials are bussin'» (lowkey means secretly; bussin' means really good): even that has one, «Lowkey», which in formal writing would be «Secretly,».
fact: An adverbial tells you when, where, how or how often something happens.
fact: A fronted adverbial is an adverbial moved to the start of a sentence.
fact: A fronted adverbial is usually followed by a comma.
fact: A fronted adverbial can be one word (Suddenly), a phrase (Under the bridge) or a clause (Because I was hungry).
fact: After a fronted adverbial, the rest must still be a complete sentence with its own subject and verb.
fact: To check, cover the fronted adverbial: what is left should make sense on its own.
fact: Fronted adverbials of time include Later that day, After lunch, At noon and Once upon a time.
fact: Fronted adverbials of place include Under the bridge, In the garden and Far away.
fact: Fronted adverbials of manner, telling how, include Carefully, Slowly and Without a sound.
fact: Some fronted adverbials show the speaker's opinion, like Frankly, Luckily and Sadly.
fact: Using a fronted adverbial in every sentence makes writing sound clunky; use them for variety.
fact: Slang: «my lover» (West Country) is a friendly way to say love or mate; «lowkey» means secretly or a bit; «bussin'» means really good; «y'all» (southern US) means you all.
exercise: «Later that day, we went home.» Which part is the fronted adverbial? | Later that day | we went home | went home | Later that day, we
exercise: A pupil writes «After lunch we played.» What is missing? | A comma after «After lunch» | A full stop after «After» | A capital letter on «played» | A question mark at the end
exercise: Grace starts every sentence of her story with a fronted adverbial. What is the problem? | It sounds clunky, so she should vary them | Nothing, more fronted adverbials always mean better writing | Fronted adverbials are not allowed in stories | Her story will have no verbs
exercise: Mum asks for an example of a fronted adverbial. Which is one? | Under the bridge, the troll waited. | The troll waited quietly under the old bridge. | The troll waited. | Under the bridge.
exercise: Ali writes «Carefully, carried the eggs.» What is wrong? | It has no subject: who carried them? | The comma should go after «carried» | «Carefully» should be «Careful» | Nothing, it is a full sentence
exercise: «Rewrite with the adverbial at the front: The bus left at noon.» | At noon, the bus left. | At noon the bus, left. | The bus, at noon, left. | Noon, at the bus left.
exercise: Tyler asks if «all of a sudden» is a fronted adverbial. When is it one? | When it starts the sentence, like «All of a sudden, the lights went out.» | Never, because it is four words long, and adverbials are always single words ending in -ly, like suddenly | Only when it ends the sentence | Only in American English
exercise: Ruby asks, «Can "Because I was hungry" be a fronted adverbial?» What is the answer? | Yes, if a main clause follows | No, because it has a verb in it | No, fronted adverbials are single words only | Yes, even if nothing follows
exercise: Dad says, «Frankly, I preferred the grammar of my day.» What does «Frankly» show? | His honest opinion | The time | The place | How fast he was speaking to them
exercise: «Lowkey, fronted adverbials are bussin'.» Which word would you use in formal writing for «Lowkey,»? | Secretly, | Bussin', | Loudly, | Lowkey-ly,
exercise: What does a fronted adverbial tell you? | When, where or how | Who did the action | What the noun is called | Whether it is a question
exercise: Which is a fronted adverbial of place? | In the garden, | Slowly, | At midnight, | Luckily,
exercise: Which is a fronted adverbial of time? | At midnight, | In the cave, | Carefully, | Frankly,
exercise: How can you check a sentence with a fronted adverbial is complete? | Cover the adverbial: the rest should make sense alone | Count the words: there must be at least ten words in it | Read it backwards | Check that it ends with «!»
exercise: What does «bussin'» mean? | Really good | Waiting for a bus | Very loud | Boring
test: «'after lunch we played' is fine, commas are optional innit». What should the pupil do? | Add a comma: «After lunch, we played.» | Leave it, commas never go after adverbials | Put the comma after «we» | Change it to a question
test: ★★ Which sentence uses a fronted adverbial correctly? | Without a sound, the cat crept upstairs. | Without a sound the cat, crept upstairs. | Without a sound, crept upstairs. | The cat crept, without a sound upstairs.
test: ★★ «Carefully Ali carried the eggs.» What is the only thing missing? | A comma after «Carefully» | The subject | A verb | A full stop after «Ali»
test: ★★★ «As soon as the bell rang, Max grabbed his bag and ran out of the door.» How long is the fronted adverbial? | Five words | One word | Three words | Six words
test: ★★★ Which sentence does NOT have a fronted adverbial? | The dog barked loudly in the night. | In the night, the dog barked loudly. | Loudly, the dog barked in the night. | Suddenly, the dog barked.
test: ★★★ Move the adverbial to the front: «The fox crept through the dark wood without making a sound.» | Without making a sound, the fox crept through the dark wood. | Without making a sound the fox, crept through the dark wood. | The fox, without making a sound crept through the dark wood. | Without, making a sound, the fox crept through the dark wood.
test: In a cartoon, the Grammar Gremlin says, «Fronted adverbials must be ONE word, like Suddenly.» What is true? | They can be words, phrases or whole clauses | Only one-word adverbials count | They must always end in -ly | They can only tell you about time
test: What would happen if every sentence in a story started «Then,»? | The story would sound repetitive and clunky | The story would sound exciting and varied | It would turn into a poem | Readers would learn lots of new words from every page
test: «my lover, what's an adverbial when it's at home?» What would be a good example to give? | Under the old bridge, the troll waited. | The troll waited under the old bridge. | The troll. | The troll waited.
test: «y'all, 'once upon a time' is a fronted adverbial?» Is it? | Yes, it tells when, and it comes first | No, it's a noun | No, it's only a title | Yes, but only because it tells you where the story happens
exam: Identify the fronted adverbial in the sentence «At noon, the omnibus departed». | At noon | the omnibus departed | departed | the omnibus
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the punctuation conventionally required after a fronted adverbial? | A comma is placed after it | A full stop is placed after it | A question mark is placed after it | A semicolon is placed before it and a colon after it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sentence «Carefully, carried the eggs». | It lacks a subject and is therefore incomplete | It is complete and correctly punctuated | It contains no verb | It contains no adverbial of any kind
exam: Ascertain which of the following may serve as a fronted adverbial. | A single word, a phrase or a subordinate clause | A single word only | A proper noun only | A complete main clause, provided that it is followed by a full stop
